CodexSection (
    schema_version: 1,
    category: "material",
    description: "M25A material codex — 50 entries. Per-material flavor text drawn from in-universe science manuals + field reports + survivor diaries. Voice: gritty + tactical + slightly noir. Specificity: real material-science numbers where possible.",
    entries: [
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_air", canonical_name: "Atmosphere — Class M", subject_id: "air", body: "Standard breathable mix is 78 percent nitrogen, 21 percent oxygen, with the remaining 1 percent split between argon, water vapor, and whatever leaked out of the last filter. Earth-baseline pressure is 101.325 kilopascals at sea level; anything under 60 kPa for sustained operation requires a pressure suit. Coalition mission planners assume two minutes of useful consciousness in vacuum. Frontier survival manuals say one. The Marauders say sixty seconds, and they hold the field record for it.", discovered_via: "tutorial"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_dirt", canonical_name: "Topsoil", subject_id: "dirt", body: "Mineral-organic regolith. Earth topsoil is 45 percent mineral grit, 25 percent water, 25 percent air, 5 percent organic. Mars topsoil is 99 percent mineral grit, 1 percent regret. Dirt is what you dig to disappear, what you pile to stop a round, what you choke on when an explosive eats a wall fifteen meters from your face. Coalition Combat Engineering Manual chapter 4 opens with the words 'dirt remains your friend' and never explains why.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_concrete", canonical_name: "Reinforced Concrete", subject_id: "concrete", body: "Portland cement plus aggregate plus rebar plus thirty years. The walls of the pre-collapse cities were poured to a 30-megapascal compressive standard. The walls of post-collapse fortifications run 12 MPa on a good day and crumble under a sustained 12.7 burst. Concrete is the material humans build with when they have given up on getting somewhere else. Frontier camps are tents. Coalition outposts are concrete. The difference is who plans to leave.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_metal_nohook", canonical_name: "Sheet Plate — Non-Grappling", subject_id: "metal_nohook", body: "Smooth-faced rolled steel plate, no penetration points, no hand-holds, no friction edges. Designed to defeat grappling-hook entry. Coalition installs it on perimeter walls of high-value targets. Frontier engineers spend whole careers learning how to defeat it with welding sparks and patience. The smooth surface reflects radar at six bands, so it also reads invisible to standard targeting until somebody walks up to it. The wall is not the security. The wall is the warning.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_hazard", canonical_name: "Hazard Substrate", subject_id: "hazard", body: "Classification marker for terrain that will harm any actor that touches it. Color-coded yellow-and-black or pulsing red depending on suit-HUD configuration. Includes contact-explosive tiles, energized floor sections, anti-personnel mines, hot reactor coolant lines. Coalition policy is to mark hazards before approaching; Frontier policy is to find out by touching. Both policies have casualty rates. The Coalition rate is published.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_loose_fill", canonical_name: "Loose Fill", subject_id: "loose_fill", body: "Granular debris, broken masonry, collapsed-rubble pile. Will not support structural loads, will not stop a round, will not seal a breach. Useful only as a delay; an actor wading through loose fill moves at 30 percent speed. Marauder ambush doctrine treats every collapsed building as a kill zone for this reason. So does Coalition counter-ambush doctrine. The difference is the direction the rounds travel.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_repair_fill", canonical_name: "Repair Foam", subject_id: "repair_fill", body: "Pre-collapse two-part polyurethane structural foam. Sets in 14 seconds at room temperature; doubles in volume; bonds to most surfaces including blood-slick steel. Coalition issues a 200-gram cartridge per soldier; Frontier hoards Coalition cartridges as currency. The foam is rated for 2 MPa compressive strength once cured, which is not concrete, but it is the difference between a hull breach and a survivable shift change.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_anchor", canonical_name: "Anchor Point", subject_id: "anchor", body: "Engineered tie-down: ringbolt, weld-seam loop, pre-fab eye. Rated for the design load of whatever is sliding past it. A 12-millimeter Coalition-spec ringbolt holds 18 kilonewtons before pulling out of the substrate; a Frontier salvaged one might hold 4. You learn which is which by jumping off the platform. Survivors prefer the Coalition spec.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_sand", canonical_name: "Granular Sand", subject_id: "sand", body: "Quartz, silica, and finer dust. Behaves as a fluid above the angle of repose, a solid below it. Mars dust is one-third the grain size of Earth sand and twice as electrostatically clingy; a Coalition trooper after eight hours outside is a walking Faraday cage. Sand will fail any seal it can reach. Sand will jam any action it can enter. Sand will outlast every empire that has ever issued a manual against it.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_wood", canonical_name: "Structural Wood", subject_id: "wood", body: "Cellulose-and-lignin biomass. Density 600 kilograms per cubic meter for kiln-dried pine; 900 for hardwood; 1100 for water-logged timbers. Wood burns at 250 Celsius surface temperature, full ignition at 300. The Frontier still builds with wood because no other material is renewable on the human timescale. The Coalition burns wooden walls preferentially because they leave the most reassuring rubble.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_glass", canonical_name: "Soda-Lime Glass", subject_id: "glass", body: "Silicon dioxide with sodium-carbonate flux, cooled past its glass-transition temperature. Brittle, transparent, and 90 percent of what stands between the inside of a habitat and the outside of the universe. A 6-millimeter pane fails at 25 megapascals; a Coalition reinforced laminate stack at 95. Glass shatters in three modes: lateral crack, radial crack, and confetti. The Marauder Tribes have a word for each. Coalition manuals use only the third one in casualty reports.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_ice", canonical_name: "Water Ice", subject_id: "ice", body: "Frozen water, crystalline phase Ih above 200 kelvin. Density 917 kg per cubic meter, less than the liquid, hence floats. On Europa it is the surface; under Europa it is the ceiling. Ice fractures along cleavage planes at minus 50 Celsius; at minus 100 it deforms plastically under sustained load. Coalition Europa expeditions plan for both modes. Frontier expeditions plan for neither and lose people. The ones who come back lose toes.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_basalt", canonical_name: "Basalt", subject_id: "basalt", body: "Iron-magnesium silicate volcanic rock. The dominant crustal material on Mars, Vulcan, and the lava-flow plains of Mimas. Density 2900 kg per cubic meter; compressive strength 200 megapascals; Mohs hardness 6. Coalition combat engineers learn that basalt cuts steel before steel cuts basalt. The Vulcan colonies make every wall and every roof and every weapon stock out of basalt. They also have an unusual amount of dental work.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_water", canonical_name: "Liquid Water", subject_id: "water", body: "Hydrogen and oxygen, two parts to one, in the fluid phase between 273 and 373 kelvin at standard pressure. The single most expensive resource on every world that does not have it for free. A Coalition Mars station prices water at 4.20 Concordat units per liter; the Frontier black market on Phobos prices it at 11. Both prices are above the cost of carbon-based life. Both numbers are negotiable, in opposite directions.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_steam", canonical_name: "Superheated Steam", subject_id: "steam", body: "Water vapor above 100 Celsius at standard pressure; above 374 Celsius and 22 megapascals it becomes a supercritical fluid that will cut steel like a torch. Coalition reactor coolant loops run at 320 Celsius and 18 MPa; a breach at full load delivers a 4-meter cone of soft-tissue removal in under a second. Engineers wear pressure-resistant smocks not for the heat but for the optics. The Frontier just paints the loops red and steps around them.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_lava", canonical_name: "Surface Lava", subject_id: "lava", body: "Molten basaltic or granitic rock, surface temperature 800 to 1200 Celsius, density 2700 kg per cubic meter, viscosity highly variable. Walking near lava is a survivable activity if you understand the thermal radiation envelope. Walking on lava is not a survivable activity at all. Vulcan colonists work in shifts of 11 minutes inside the heat envelope; longer than that and you exit the shift on a gurney, less skin than you came in with.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_oil", canonical_name: "Lubricating Oil", subject_id: "oil", body: "Refined petroleum or synthetic-ester fluid for machinery bearings, mech actuators, and weapon actions. Coalition specs require a viscosity index above 130 and an autoignition point above 380 Celsius. Frontier oil is whatever drained out of the last truck. A weapon dry-fired without oil binds within 40 rounds; a chassis joint dry-fired galls within 200 cycles. The cheapest substance on the battlefield kills you the slowest.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_fire", canonical_name: "Combustion Flame", subject_id: "fire", body: "Self-sustaining exothermic oxidation. Visible band 600 to 1400 Celsius depending on fuel. The Husks make fire deliberately. The Marauders make fire accidentally. The Coalition makes fire when the manual says to and not otherwise. The flame envelope on a Coalition-issue thermite charge is 2.4 meters wide at first second, 1.1 meters at 12 seconds, 0 at 60 seconds. The hole it leaves is permanent.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_smoke", canonical_name: "Particulate Smoke", subject_id: "smoke", body: "Suspended combustion products: soot, water vapor, carbon monoxide, partially-oxidized hydrocarbons. Reduces visibility to under 4 meters at moderate density. Coalition smoke grenades produce a calibrated mix of zinc oxide and hexachloroethane that hangs at chest height for 45 seconds. Marauder smoke is whatever they set on fire when the situation got loud. Both work. Only one comes off the uniform.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_ash", canonical_name: "Combustion Ash", subject_id: "ash", body: "What remains when a thing has finished being a thing. Alkaline, hygroscopic, dust-fine. The volcanic plains of Vulcan are knee-deep in basaltic ash from the last eruption cycle, which was 80 years ago and is overdue. Ash will scour an optical lens in 12 hours of wind exposure. Coalition optics wear sacrificial sapphire covers. Frontier optics wear electrical tape and hope. Both blind eventually.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_acid", canonical_name: "Concentrated Acid Pool", subject_id: "acid", body: "Sulfuric, nitric, or mixed-process industrial acid, concentrations above 70 percent. pH below 1. Will dissolve cotton in seconds, leather in minutes, mild steel in hours. A Coalition acid-resistant coating buys 45 seconds of contact survival; a Frontier rubber poncho buys 8. Venus has lakes of the stuff at atmospheric pressure 90 times Earth's. The Marauders use small jars of it as field weapons. The Husks bathe in it.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_acid_fumes", canonical_name: "Acid Vapor", subject_id: "acid_fumes", body: "Airborne acid mist; pH under 2; will corrode mucosal tissue in the lungs in seconds of unprotected exposure. Coalition gas-masks rated to 50 ppm; Venus surface workers wear full pressure suits because the ambient concentration is 4000. A Frontier scavenger working a Venus wreck without a sealed mask gets twelve breaths before pulmonary edema starts. The autopsy reports use the word 'foam' more than once.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_rust", canonical_name: "Oxide Coating", subject_id: "rust", body: "Iron-oxide corrosion on ferrous metals, expansion factor 6.4 by volume, structural-failure mode 'flaking'. A rusted Coalition weapon may fire one full magazine before something internal binds permanently. A rusted Frontier weapon will fire half a magazine. A rusted Marauder weapon will fire. The difference between the three is that the Marauder weapon was already broken when issued. Rust is not weakness; rust is honesty.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_blood", canonical_name: "Hemoglobin Fluid — Class A", subject_id: "blood", body: "Iron-based oxygen-transport fluid, average circulating volume 5 liters in a 75 kg human. Class A blood loss begins at 750 milliliters lost. Class B at 1500 mL. Death without intervention at 2500 mL or 30 percent of body volume. Coalition combat-medic doctrine focuses entirely on slowing the loss; the Collegium focuses on synthesizing the replacement. Both succeed. Neither helps if the loss is fast.", discovered_via: "first_kill"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_synth_blood", canonical_name: "Hemolymph — Class S", subject_id: "synth_blood", body: "Synthetic oxygen-transport fluid for android-class actors. Hyperbaric perfluorocarbon emulsion in a phosphate buffer, color pale violet, viscosity 1.4 centipoise at 37 Celsius. Synth bleeds slower than human; synth dies faster from coolant loss. Coalition field manuals have separate triage flowcharts for Class A and Class S fluid. The Collegium argues these should be combined. The Coalition has not changed the manual in 18 years.", discovered_via: "first_kill"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_synth_oil", canonical_name: "Mech Hydraulic Fluid", subject_id: "synth_oil", body: "Anti-shear silicone-ester fluid for powered-armor actuators. Operating range minus 60 to plus 240 Celsius. Density 970 kg per cubic meter. Color is dye-pack identification — Coalition runs amber, Frontier runs olive, Synth Collective runs colorless. A chassis hit on the hydraulic reservoir leaks at 0.4 liters per second on the high-pressure side; the operator has 12 seconds before the limb seizes. Twelve seconds is a long time in a firefight. Until it isn't.", discovered_via: "first_kill"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_bio_fluid", canonical_name: "Husk Bioslurry", subject_id: "bio_fluid", body: "The fluid that fills a Husk after the Husk has finished being something else. Composition unclassified by Coalition labs because no sample stays stable long enough to analyze; the substance ferments on contact with atmospheric oxygen and produces a vapor that triggers olfactory hallucinations in unprotected observers. Frontier scavengers say it smells like burnt sugar and old paper. Coalition specs require the immediate area to be flushed with foam.", discovered_via: "first_kill"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_molten_steel", canonical_name: "Molten Steel", subject_id: "molten_steel", body: "Iron-carbon alloy above its melting point of 1370 Celsius. Glows red at 700, orange at 900, yellow-white at 1200, fully transparent to thermal imaging across all bands. Coalition reactor breach scenarios assume 2 cubic meters of molten steel in the primary containment loop; a full release floods the lower deck and renders the deck unusable for the remainder of the operation. Steel does not extinguish. Steel only cools.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_molten_glass", canonical_name: "Molten Glass", subject_id: "molten_glass", body: "Soda-lime glass above 700 Celsius, viscous and slow-flowing. Hits the floor and forms a 4-centimeter pool that stays liquid for 30 seconds and stays hot for 11 minutes. Used in Coalition heavy-incendiary munitions because the glass adheres to any surface and continues radiating heat after the explosion is over. Marauders call it 'sticky sun'. The Coalition manual calls it 'thermal pacification ordnance'. The casualty reports use the Marauder term.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_magma_basaltic", canonical_name: "Basaltic Magma", subject_id: "magma_basaltic", body: "Low-silica subsurface molten basalt, viscosity 100 to 1000 pascal-seconds, temperature 1100 to 1250 Celsius. Flows like motor oil at full heat. The dominant intrusion in the Vulcan crust; Coalition geological surveys map basaltic magma chambers down to 4 kilometers below surface. A breach in a basaltic chamber wall floods a tunnel at 40 meters per minute. You do not outrun magma. You outplan it.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_magma_granitic", canonical_name: "Granitic Magma", subject_id: "magma_granitic", body: "High-silica subsurface molten granite, viscosity 10000 to 100000 pascal-seconds, temperature 650 to 900 Celsius. Flows like cold tar. Forms a glassy skin within seconds of atmospheric contact. The Marauder Tribes carve weapons from granitic obsidian extracted from cooled flows; the edge is harder than steel and shatters on the third hit. The Coalition issues steel knives that bend on the second hit and don't shatter on any. Choose your fragility.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_metal_reforged", canonical_name: "Reforged Plate", subject_id: "metal_reforged", body: "Scavenged hull plating remelted and rerolled, typically by Frontier salvage forges. Yield strength 180 megapascals on average, with a 60 MPa standard deviation that engineers describe as 'character'. A reforged plate stops a 5.56 round at 60 percent the rate of a Coalition-spec plate. The price differential is 90 percent. The Frontier considers this a fair trade. The Coalition does not.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_obsidian", canonical_name: "Volcanic Obsidian", subject_id: "obsidian", body: "Amorphous silica glass, formed from rapidly cooled granitic magma. Conchoidal fracture, Mohs hardness 5 to 5.5, edge sharpness measured in single-digit nanometers — sharper than any honed steel. Marauder warbands carve obsidian into single-use ambush blades; the blade survives one cut and the cut survives forever. The Coalition has classified the resulting wound geometry. The classification number is 7.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_snow", canonical_name: "Crystalline Snowpack", subject_id: "snow", body: "Hexagonal-platelet water-ice precipitation, density 100 kg per cubic meter for fresh fall, 400 for packed, 800 for ice-layer compacted. Insulates at 0.05 watts per meter-kelvin — better than fiberglass batt. Europa snowpack on the surface is half methane crystals by volume; lighting a match through it is a 4-meter casualty. Coalition Europa manuals prohibit open flame within 12 meters of snowpack. Frontier manuals don't prohibit it. Frontier Europa casualty rates reflect.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_mud", canonical_name: "Slurry Mud", subject_id: "mud", body: "Water-saturated fine sediment, viscosity variable, shear strength variable, suction force on a boot variable. The single most efficient stopping medium in infantry warfare. Coalition manuals recommend avoiding it. Marauder ambush doctrine recommends choosing terrain that maximizes it. A trooper sunk to mid-thigh extracts in 90 seconds with help; in 11 minutes without. The Marauders do not show up until 8.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_baked_clay", canonical_name: "Fired Clay", subject_id: "baked_clay", body: "Kiln-fired ceramic, compressive strength 50 MPa, useful only as architectural fill and decorative skin. The Religious Order Collegium fires its statuary in clay; a 4-meter clay icon on a temple plinth absorbs 12 rounds of 5.56 before the head separates. Coalition spec on temple raids includes a flowchart for managing the icon-collapse psychological consequences. The flowchart has six steps. None of them work.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_salt", canonical_name: "Crystalline Salt", subject_id: "salt", body: "Sodium chloride, halite mineral form, hygroscopic, abundant on dried seabeds across every habitable world. Useful for preservation, electrolyte balance, and rendering enemy mech actuators inoperable through saltwater exposure. The Frontier sea-salt mines on the Belt asteroids exported 11000 tonnes last fiscal year. The Pirates intercepted 3000 of them. The Coalition recovered 400 from a single Pirate wreck. The remainder is on Mars somewhere and trades at 14 to 1.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_molten_salt", canonical_name: "Molten Salt", subject_id: "molten_salt", body: "Sodium chloride above its melting point of 801 Celsius. Used in Coalition Sol-zone breeder reactors as a heat-transfer medium; the Helios Pact uses it as a baptismal fluid. A reactor breach in a molten-salt loop releases vapor that crystallizes in the lungs in 4 seconds. The Pact ritual has eleven attendants and a 12 percent fatality rate. Both groups consider the salt holy. Both groups have separate reasons.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_brine", canonical_name: "Saturated Brine", subject_id: "brine", body: "Sodium-chloride solution near saturation, density 1.2 kg per liter. The dominant subsurface liquid on Europa below the ice cap. Coalition Europa drill cores show brine pockets 200 meters deep with conductivity high enough to short a chassis if waded; Frontier salvagers strip electrical insulation off Coalition charts and sell the maps. The Pirates buy them. The Pirates lose chassis to brine pockets. The cycle is closed.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_brine_ice", canonical_name: "Brine Ice", subject_id: "brine_ice", body: "Frozen brine, crystalline structure shot through with salt-pocket inclusions. Found on Europa surface and in the deep Belt; weaker than fresh-water ice by about 30 percent. Coalition rated for foot traffic at 12 cm thickness; for chassis at 24 cm; for vehicles never. The reason every Belt outpost has a footbridge instead of a road. The reason every Belt outpost has lost a vehicle into the brine. The reason every Belt outpost has a salvage budget.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_gunpowder", canonical_name: "Smokeless Propellant", subject_id: "gunpowder", body: "Nitrocellulose-based single-base or double-base smokeless powder, burn rate calibrated to weapon chamber pressure. A Coalition 5.56 cartridge holds 1.8 grams of powder; deflagration produces a peak pressure of 380 megapascals in 0.3 milliseconds. The Marauders reload spent casings with whatever grade of powder fell off the back of the last supply truck. Marauder weapons jam at 1 in 18 rounds. Coalition weapons jam at 1 in 1100. Both jam in the same way. Only one of them happens to you.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_thermite", canonical_name: "Thermite Charge", subject_id: "thermite", body: "Aluminum-iron oxide pyrotechnic, burns at 2500 Celsius, self-sustaining once initiated. Will cut through 25 millimeters of mild steel in 8 seconds. Coalition issues 200-gram thermite charges for door breaching, hardpoint disablement, and serial-number obliteration of recovered Coalition equipment that was about to be recovered by other parties. The Frontier doesn't issue thermite; the Frontier just steals it. The supply chain pretends to be confused.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_plasma", canonical_name: "Ionized Plasma", subject_id: "plasma", body: "Gas-state matter at temperatures high enough to ionize electrons free from atomic nuclei. Coalition plasma-weapon containment magnets hold a 10000-Kelvin plasma column at 30 millimeter diameter for 0.4 seconds before delivery; the column will cut a Frontier armor plate cleanly and reseal the entry hole behind it with re-solidified slag. Plasma kills neat. The reports describe the entrance wound only.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_methane", canonical_name: "Methane Gas", subject_id: "methane", body: "Single-carbon alkane, formula CH4, flammable across a wide stoichiometric range at standard pressure. The dominant volatile on Mimas and the lower Belt; Coalition surveyors price liquefied methane at 0.40 Concordat per liter and ship it inward as feedstock. A methane leak detected by sense of smell is already explosive. The Coalition issues helmet-air sensors. The Frontier issues canaries. The Pirates issue prayers.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_liquid_methane", canonical_name: "Liquid Methane", subject_id: "liquid_methane", body: "Cryogenic-condensed methane, density 422 kg per cubic meter at 90 kelvin. The lakes and rivers of Mimas. Walking on the shore is survivable in a properly rated cold-suit; falling in is a 4-minute death-march to the surface assuming you can swim, which you cannot, because the suit floats and the lake doesn't care. Coalition Mimas survival is 89 percent over a 30-day deployment. Frontier survival is 71. Both are recruiting.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_hydrogen", canonical_name: "Hydrogen Gas", subject_id: "hydrogen", body: "Diatomic hydrogen, H2, lightest gas in the universe, flammable down to 4 percent by volume in air. Coalition fuel-cell APCs run on hydrogen because the byproduct is water, and water is currency. A hydrogen leak on a Coalition APC has a 4 percent ignition rate; the Frontier figure is 9; the Pirate figure is 'always'. Hydrogen flame is invisible in daylight. You learn the leak by stepping into it.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_liquid_hydrogen", canonical_name: "Liquid Hydrogen", subject_id: "liquid_hydrogen", body: "Cryogenic-condensed hydrogen, 20 kelvin, density 71 kg per cubic meter. Useful only as rocket propellant and reactor fuel. Coalition orbital staging requires 4 tonnes per launch; a single tank costs more than a Frontier APC platoon. Liquid hydrogen will fast-freeze human tissue in 0.4 seconds of contact and the tissue will shatter into crystalline fragments on impact with anything. The Coalition manual classifies this as 'industrial accident' for paperwork purposes.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_oxygen", canonical_name: "Oxygen Gas", subject_id: "oxygen", body: "Diatomic oxygen, O2, comprises 21 percent of breathable atmosphere. The currency of every off-world habitat. A Coalition Mars station produces 4 tonnes per day through electrolysis. The Frontier black market on Phobos prices an emergency oxygen cylinder at 80 hours of station labor. A Marauder Tribe will fight for an empty cylinder, on the chance there's a slow leak left in it. There usually isn't.", discovered_via: "tutorial"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_liquid_oxygen", canonical_name: "Liquid Oxygen", subject_id: "liquid_oxygen", body: "Cryogenic-condensed oxygen, 90 kelvin, density 1141 kg per cubic meter, pale-blue in color. Will turn any organic material into a fuel-air explosive on contact. The Coalition does not store LOX adjacent to fuel; the Frontier sometimes does; the Pirates always do. The investigation into the Belt-7 fuel-depot blast of 2118 concluded the cause was 'storage adjacency' and the recommendation was 'do not'. The depot was not rebuilt.", discovered_via: "first_visit"),
        CodexEntry (id: "codex_material_nitrogen", canonical_name: "Nitrogen Gas", subject_id: "nitrogen", body: "Diatomic nitrogen, N2, 78 percent of breathable atmosphere, chemically inert under ordinary conditions. A Coalition habitat uses N2 as a fire-suppression flood: pump the room with N2 to 16 percent oxygen and the fire stops. The occupants also stop. Coalition emergency protocol requires a 30-second alarm before the flood. The Frontier protocol uses a 5-second alarm. The Pirates skip the alarm.", discovered_via: "first_use"),
    ]
)
